INTRODUCTION = [
    'Railway overhead contact systems (OCS) depend on technical requirements distributed across standards for components, interfaces, inspection and construction. A useful document query must identify the relevant provision and preserve its numerical value, unit, qualification and edition. A fluent explanation can still be unsuitable when it combines requirements from different documents or omits the condition under which a value applies. The engineering problem addressed here is therefore the traceability of a retrieved answer within a selected document collection.',
    'Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) combines an external evidence collection with language generation [1]. BM25 supplies a lexical baseline weighted by matching terms [2]. Dense retrieval represents query and passage meaning in a vector space [3]. Their rankings can differ for component names, identifiers, paraphrases and conditions. Combining scores is a testable choice whose benefit depends on the corpus and questions.',
    'Query rewriting can help express an information need [4]. This project uses rules for topic inheritance in simple follow-ups. Distractor studies motivate testing evidence sufficiency [5]. RAGAs separates retrieval and answer assessment [6]. Here, correctness and semantic support are assessed against source documents through a recorded assistant review procedure. Dense retrieval uses the multilingual BGE-M3 encoder [7].',
    'Regulatory text retrieval has combined BM25 with a fine-tuned sentence encoder [8]. A railway engineering case study illustrates retrieval-augmented consultation of UNISIG specifications [9]. VersionRAG models changes between document versions in a graph [10]. For Chinese OCS standards, this project retains explicit standard numbers, editions and original page locations through the answer pipeline. Its controlled evaluation tests how much lexical matching, semantic ranking, edition checks and evidence screening contribute within the selected corpus.',
    'The project develops an OCS document-learning and consultation system around DeepSeek generation, BAAI/bge-m3 embeddings, external application programming interfaces (APIs) and a local Streamlit interface. Its research contribution is a controlled comparison of retrieval, edition constraints and a heuristic Evidence Gate within an auditable pipeline. The research questions are whether Hybrid improves evidence recall over BM25 and Dense, whether edition filtering reduces answers based on a non-requested edition, and whether the Evidence Gate reduces unsupported answers while maintaining acceptable refusal behaviour.',
]

METHOD = [
    ('h2', 'A. Corpus and evidence provenance'),
    ('p', 'The study collection contains 25 independently numbered documents and 3341 text chunks. Source hashes, standard identifiers, years and file locations accompany each document. Eleven documents are individually numbered parts extracted from a bound collection. Their original page ranges and offsets are recorded, and their derived pages were compared with the corresponding parent pages. This permits a citation in a derived file to be traced back to the source collection. The count follows independently numbered document identities.'),
    ('p', 'PDF documents are parsed by file-page and DOCX material by paragraph or table row. The chunking setting is 600 characters with an 80-character overlap, while local source units are retained in the stored provenance. Exact file hashing prevents duplicate imports. Scanned, unusable or draft-like material was excluded during screening. Text extraction preserves evidence for inspection but may introduce spacing or character errors. The benchmark review therefore checks numerical tokens, units, inequality signs and surrounding conditions against the original document.'),
    ('p', 'The intended two-family, two-formal-edition collection was unavailable. A file labelled as a 2018 edition contained unresolved date placeholders and was conservatively excluded. No formal edition pair was admitted to the final collection. RQ2 therefore evaluates the behaviour for available and absent requested editions within this corpus. A question requesting an absent year expresses an input condition and does not establish that a formal standard with that year exists.'),
    ('h2', 'B. Retrieval and edition constraints'),
    ('p', 'Indexing, query processing and lexical evidence checks share jieba precise tokenisation with HMM disabled, a small frozen domain dictionary and fixed stopwords. Standard identifiers, technical terms, numbers and units remain available for indexing. BM25 uses k1 = 1.5 and b = 0.75. Dense retrieval uses raw cosine similarity between 1024-dimensional BAAI/bge-m3 query and chunk vectors [7]. The vector endpoint and model identity are stored, and a model change invalidates the corresponding index.'),
    ('p', 'Hybrid combines separately normalised BM25 and Dense scores over the eligible candidate set. For a query, min–max normalisation maps a variable score range to zero through one. A constant positive score maps to one and any other constant score maps to zero. The combined score is S = (1 − w)B + wD, where B and D are the normalised scores. Stable chunk identifiers break ranking ties. All three retrieval methods return at most five passages, allowing the same Recall@5 criterion to be applied.'),
    ('p', 'Before ranking, explicit standard-family and year requirements constrain the candidate set. An unavailable requested year produces an explicit non-answer and an available-year list. A family with several available years requires clarification if no year is specified. Conflicting explicit and bare edition cues also require clarification. For simple follow-ups, the previous resolved user question supplies the topic when the current question uses a referential expression. An explicit new standard replaces that topic. Model responses are excluded from the factual evidence used for this resolution.'),
    ('h2', 'C. Evidence Gate and answer validation'),
    ('p', 'The Evidence Gate retains a passage when either its lexical coverage or its raw cosine similarity reaches the selected threshold. Lexical coverage is the number of distinct query content tokens found in the passage divided by all distinct query content tokens. Standard identifiers and fixed stopwords are removed for this calculation, and an empty content-token set scores zero. BM25 uses the lexical signal because it does not request a query embedding. The signals estimate surface correspondence and retrieval similarity, while semantic sufficiency remains a source-review judgement.'),
    ('p', 'Only retained evidence is sent to DeepSeek. The prompt requests structured atomic claims with source numbers and continuous quoted spans, preserves requested editions and instructs the model to explain missing evidence. Temperature is zero and the configured maximum output length is fixed before evaluation. Output parsing verifies that every displayed claim has a citation, the source number belongs to the supplied evidence, and the quoted span occurs in that source. Failed parsing, unsupported citation identifiers and mismatched excerpts produce explicit error states. These checks support traceability while allowing a fluent, correctly quoted claim to remain semantically wrong.'),
    ('p', 'SQLite stores documents, chunks and vectors transactionally. Streamlit displays answers, evidence and PDF pages. Online-processing permissions and API errors are recorded. Vector batches and first-attempt slots persist individually. Interrupted requests with unavailable results remain unknown failed outcomes, retaining their place in the primary analysis.'),
]

EVALUATION = [
    ('h2', 'A. Benchmark and review procedure'),
    ('p', 'The benchmark contains 100 questions. Development uses 20 questions, comprising 16 answerable and four unanswerable items. The held-out test uses 80 questions, comprising 64 answerable and 16 unanswerable items. Development category counts are six clause queries, three terminology queries, four edition queries, three follow-ups and four unanswerable queries. The corresponding test counts are 24, 12, 16, 12 and 16. Unanswerable items equally represent absent editions, out-of-corpus domain information, unrelated requests and ambiguity.'),
    ('p', 'Each answerable record contains a reference answer, one to three required facts and source evidence units identified by document, location and continuous excerpt. Unanswerable records state their reason and expected refusal or clarification. Related facts and conversations are grouped within one split. Review covers the actual source wording and the compatibility of the question, necessary facts and expected action. Exact source matching and quota checks supplement this review. The question set remains concentrated in a subset of the collection, which limits its representativeness.'),
    ('p', 'The student authorised the assistant to complete question, passage and output review. Records identify this procedure as assistant_source_review and preserve the actual execution times and source-based reasons. Two source-review passes were recorded for the questions. This changes the proposal\'s planned human annotation. Independent student, expert and inter-rater validation remain unmeasured. Shared assistance in benchmark construction and scoring may create correlated errors, so the resulting quality scores should be interpreted as corpus-bound, assistant-reviewed measurements.'),
    ('h2', 'B. Configuration selection and controlled runs'),
    ('p', 'Only development data select parameters. The candidate weights are 0.25, 0.50 and 0.75, ordered by Recall@5 and then MRR@5 on the 16 answerable development items. Remaining ties favour 0.50, then 0.25, then 0.75. Gate calibration considers lexical thresholds 0.05, 0.10 and 0.20 and cosine thresholds 0.20, 0.30, 0.40 and 0.50. The selection maximises mean passage F1 across the three retrieval modes, followed by fewer question-level false refusals, higher precision and the fixed grid order.'),
    ('p', 'The five configurations are B1 BM25, B2 Dense, B3 Hybrid, A1 Hybrid with edition checks disabled, and A2 Hybrid with the retrieval gate disabled. A1 retains standard-family filtering. A2 retains prompts, model refusal, citation validation and error handling. A development integration pass precedes freezing. The formal evaluation then allocates 80 fixed questions to each configuration, giving 400 primary first-attempt slots. A slot may terminate before generation through a version or evidence check. Recovery attempts, where required, are stored separately.'),
    ('h2', 'C. Measures and uncertainty'),
    ('p', 'Recall@5 and mean reciprocal rank (MRR@5) use the pre-gate ranking over all 64 answerable test questions. Recall is the macro-average proportion of each question\'s gold evidence units covered in the top five. MRR averages the reciprocal rank of its first correct evidence hit, with zero for no hit. A gold match requires document identity, location and excerpt coverage. Full answer correctness also uses 64 as its denominator, retaining errors and refusals as failures. Citation support counts supported cited claims over all displayed cited claims, judging the citation set jointly. Citation coverage and resolvability are reported separately. Wrong-edition rate uses all unambiguous specified-edition items, including missing editions. Refusal recall uses the 16 unanswerable items, and false refusal uses the 64 answerable items.'),
    ('p', 'Unsupported-answer incidence uses all 80 test items and is accompanied by its conditional rate among substantive answers and by answer coverage. API, format and citation errors are reported separately from justified refusals. Zero denominators remain unavailable. Retrieval, generation and end-to-end timing are summarised by median and nearest-rank 95th percentile (P95). Paired differences use group_id resampling with seed 3801 and 2000 bootstrap draws. Questions in a sampled group remain together on both sides of each comparison. The small number of source groups limits the stability and generalisation of these intervals.'),
]

